<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Fun Outdoors!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like to Swim.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Express basic needs and feelings using simple phrases. Interactive: Interact in a predictable pattern with simple questions and answers about familiar things using simple phrases.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe personal preferences and feelings for activities using simple sentences (e.g., "I love to swim."). Can ask and answer predictable questions about preferences (e.g., "Do you like to ride bikes?" "Yes, I like it."). Can express basic needs to peers using simple phrases related to outdoor activities (e.g., "You need to run." or "You need to jump.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Guess the Action" game. The teacher shows flashcards of outdoor activities (e.g., a child swimming, running, jumping, riding a bike, playing soccer) and performs the action. Students say the corresponding verb or short phrase (e.g., "Swim!" "Run fast!"). This activates prior knowledge of vocabulary from previous lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models sentences expressing preferences and actions. The teacher holds up the 'swim' flashcard and says, "I like to swim. I swim slowly." Then, the teacher holds up the 'run' flashcard and says, "I like to run. I run fast." The teacher exaggerates the /s/ sound in "swim" and the /r/ sound in "run" while modeling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple questions and examples.
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I like to swim?" (Students answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'fast' for running or swimming quickly?" (Students answer: "Running quickly!" or "Running!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a 'jump' flashcard) "Do I like to jump high?" (Students answer: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Fun Outdoors!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, brightly illustrated poster or projected image displaying a short dialogue titled "Friends at the Park!" between two children (e.g., Sam and Ana). The dialogue features simple sentences about outdoor activities and preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sam: "Hi, Ana! Do you like to play at the park?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ana: "Yes, I love to run fast!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sam: "I like to ride my bike. It's fun!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ana: "Me too! Do you like to swim?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sam: "Yes, I love to swim slowly."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Express basic preferences for outdoor activities and interact in a predictable pattern with simple questions and answers about familiar things.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the dialogue aloud, pointing to each character.
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What do Sam and Ana like to do outside?" (Expected answer: "Play at the park," "Run," "Bike," "Swim")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> "Point to the person who likes to run fast." "Point to the person who likes to ride a bike."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Sam likes to jump." (Students indicate True/False with thumbs up/down). "Ana loves to run." (Students indicate True/False).</li>
                    </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice expressing preferences for activities using "I like to [verb]." and "I love to [verb]." and asking "Do you like to [verb]?" They will use verbs (run, jump, swim, play, ride) and adverbs (fast, slowly) and adjectives (fun, happy). Pronunciation practice on /r/ and /s/ sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Choral and Individual Repetition with TPR):</b> The teacher uses flashcards for outdoor activities. The teacher shows a card (e.g., 'swimming') and says, "I like to swim. I swim slowly." Students repeat chorally, then the teacher points to individual students to repeat. Repeat for other activities (run fast, jump high, play soccer, ride bike). Emphasize /r/ and /s/ sounds.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Builder Cards):</b> Students are given simple sentence starter cards like "I like to _______." and picture cards of verbs and adverbs (e.g., 'run', 'swim', 'jump', 'fast', 'slowly'). In pairs, students choose a verb and an adverb and complete the sentence aloud to their partner, e.g., "I like to run fast." Then, they switch. The teacher monitors for correct use of present simple and adverbs.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Question and Answer Exchange):</b> Students work in pairs. Student A asks, "Do you like to [activity, e.g., 'swim']?" Student B answers, "Yes, I like to swim!" or "No, I don't." Then they switch roles. The teacher provides sentence frames on the board for support: "Do you like to _______?" "Yes, I like to _______!" "No, I don't."</li>
                    </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Fun Outdoors!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Outdoor Activity Interview." Students will work in small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a small set of picture cards depicting various outdoor activities (e.g., swim, run, jump, play soccer, ride bike, climb, watch a game). Each student will take turns asking and answering questions about their preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students sit in their groups. Student 1 picks an activity card (e.g., 'swimming') and asks Student 2, "Do you like to swim?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 2 answers, "Yes, I love to swim!" or "No, I don't." If they like it, they can add "It's fun!" or "I swim slowly." if possible.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 2 then picks a new card and asks Student 3. The activity continues until all cards have been used and everyone has had a chance to ask and answer multiple times.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing fluency, encouraging interaction, and providing soft scaffolding or rephrasing as needed, focusing on communication rather than perfect accuracy.</li>
                    </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Outdoor Activity Interview" (Stage 4). The teacher uses a simple checklist to note students' ability to ask and answer questions and use target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will perform the "Outdoor Activity Interview" with a partner for the teacher or another pair. Each student asks 2 questions and answers 2 questions about outdoor activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear production of /r/ in "run" and /s/ in "swim."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Employs words like "run," "jump," "swim," "like," "love," "bike," "fast," "fun."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Asking questions:</b> Correctly forms "Do you like to [activity]?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Answering questions:</b> Responds appropriately with "Yes, I like it!" or "No, I don't."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Expressing preferences:</b> Attempts to add "It's fun!" or simple descriptions like "I run fast."</li>
                    </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket before leaving.
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Draw a happy face if you could ask "Do you like to...?" and a sad face if it was hard.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Draw a happy face if you could say "I like to..." and a sad face if it was hard.</li>
                    </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection and connection to next lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new outdoor activities did I talk about today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who did I ask about their favorite activity?"</li>
                    </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What speaking activities worked best for engaging 2nd graders?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to use "I like/love to..." and "Do you like...?" accurately? What challenges did they face with pronunciation or sentence structure?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I better scaffold the use of adverbs (fast, slowly) and adjectives (fun) in spoken sentences for Pre-A1.4 learners in the next speaking opportunity?</li>
                    </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on speaking about preferences for outdoor activities. In Lesson 4 (Writing), students will transfer this oral practice into simple written sentences, describing their own and their friends' favorite outdoor activities using the target vocabulary and grammar structures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>